        "content": "<p>Tau Ceti has just <a href=\"#narrow/channel/610393-Tau-Ceti/topic/PRs/near/626806975\">merged</a> a proposed complete <a href=\"https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/main/TauCeti/GroupTheory/SpecificGroups/CFSG/Classification.lean#L50\">statement</a> of the classification of finite simple groups. To my knowledge, this is the first time the full statement has been formalized.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ClassificationStatement</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Finite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsSimpleGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">],</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CFSGIndex</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I've also added this statement as a problem on LeanEval: <a href=\"https://lean-lang.org/eval/problems/classification_finite_simple_groups/\">https://lean-lang.org/eval/problems/classification_finite_simple_groups/</a>.</p>\n<p>(I'll send a Lean t-shirt to whoever finds mistakes in the statement and its prerequisites in Tau Ceti, serious enough to make the LeanEval problem probably false!)</p>\n<p>There's lots more to be done separately from the actual classification, e.g. proving finiteness and simplicity of the groups constructed here (there's a <a href=\"https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCetiRoadmap/pull/446\">TauCetiRoadmap#446</a> PR for this), and proving some basic facts about all the groups to gain confidence we've specified them correctly. It would be also nice to have a classification statement that says our Lie type groups really are exactly the simple groups <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[G^F, G^F] / Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0913em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span></span></span></span> arising from Steinberg endomorphisms <code>F</code> of simply connected simple algebraic groups <code>G</code> over <code>𝔽̄_p</code>. (<a href=\"https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCetiRoadmap/pull/447\">TauCetiRoadmap#447</a>) Already we use the language of reductive groups to set up these examples.</p>",
